y army who led resistance in Guadeloupe to the restoration of slavery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-edouard-bouet-willaumez"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-edouard-bouet-willaumez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez was a French naval officer and colonial administrator who explored and charted the West African coast in the 1830s and 1840s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-fadrique"><span class="enc-name">Louis Fadrique</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Fadrique was count of Salona in central Greece and a member of the Fadrique family, a branch of the royal house of Sicily.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-faidherbe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Faidherbe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Faidherbe was a French general and colonial governor of Senegal from 1854 to 1861 and 1863 to 1865.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-of-etruria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I of Etruria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I was the first king of Etruria, a son of Duke Ferdinand of Parma.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I of Hungary</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I of Hungary, known as Louis the Great, was king of Hungary and Croatia from 1342 and king of Poland from 1370 until his death in 1382.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-count-of-flanders"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Count of Flanders</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, known as Louis of Nevers, was count of Flanders, Nevers and Rethel from 1322 until his death at the Battle of Crécy in 1346.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-bavaria"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, called the Kelheimer, was Duke of Bavaria of the house of Wittelsbach from 1183 to 1231.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-duke-of-orleans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, Duke of Orléans, was the younger brother of King Charles VI of France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-grand-duke-of-hesse"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-grand-duke-of-hesse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Grand Duke of Hesse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I was the last landgrave of Hesse-Darmstadt, as Louis X from 1790, and the first grand duke of Hesse.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen from 1603 until his death, was the first ruler of the separate principality of Anhalt-Köthen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II de La Trémoille</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II de La Trémoille was a French commander who served Charles VIII, Louis XII and Francis I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Hungary</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Hungary was the Jagiellonian king of Hungary and Bohemia from 1516.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Italy was the eldest son of Lothair I, king of Italy from 844 and Carolingian emperor from 855.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-poitiers"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Poitiers</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Poitiers was the last count of Valentinois and Diois in the Rhône valley, of the Poitiers family that had ruled there since the 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Count of Flanders</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, known as Louis of Male, was count of Flanders from 1346 to 1384 and also count of Nevers and Rethel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, Duke of Bavaria, known as the Strict, was a Wittelsbach duke and Count Palatine of the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon, called the Good Duke, was a French prince who fought in the Hundred Years' War and led a crusade to Mahdia in 1390.</span></a></li></ul>
